---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import Closing from '../../components/Closing.astro';

/**
 * There is no vendor here and therefore no verified-date table: a spreadsheet
 * is a method, not a product. Nothing on this page describes a competitor's
 * features, so there is nothing to date-stamp. Do not invent a comparison
 * table of "Excel features" — that would be a claim about Microsoft.
 */

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is a spreadsheet good enough for receipts?',
    a: 'A spreadsheet is an excellent ledger. It records what you typed. What it cannot do is be the receipt — if someone asks to see the document behind row 47, the spreadsheet has nothing to show. That is the gap, and it is not a gap you can close by typing more carefully.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use both?',
    a: 'Yes, and plenty of drivers do. Keep the running total in the sheet if that is how you think; keep the images in CabSnap so the paper behind each line still exists. They are not competing — one is arithmetic, the other is evidence.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I get my data out of CabSnap into a spreadsheet?',
    a: 'Check the current app listing on Google Play for exactly what export exists today, rather than trusting a claim typed on a marketing page. We would rather you see the real feature list than our description of it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the IRS accept photos of receipts?',
    a: 'Yes. Under Rev. Proc. 97-22 the IRS accepts electronic copies of paper records, provided the image is legible, matches the original, can be printed on request, and is stored securely. This is a recordkeeping answer, not tax advice — talk to your tax preparer about your own situation.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="CabSnap vs a Spreadsheet — Keep the Proof"
  description="A spreadsheet records what you typed. It needs the receipt to still exist. That's the failure point, and it happens in a hot cab in about six weeks."
  path="/vs/spreadsheets"
  faq={faq}
  narrow
>
  <section class="hero dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow orange">Comparison</p>
      <h1>CabSnap<br />vs a <em>spreadsheet</em></h1>
      <p class="lead measure">
        A spreadsheet is a good ledger. It is not a receipt. That distinction
        stays invisible until the day somebody asks to see one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">What CabSnap is for</p>
      <h2>A spreadsheet is a ledger.<br />CabSnap keeps the <em>proof</em>.</h2>
      <p class="intro measure">
        CabSnap captures the actual receipt behind every expense &mdash; snap it,
        it's read, filed, and searchable.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The honest part</p>
      <h2>A spreadsheet is<br />a real system.</h2>
      <p class="intro measure">
        A spreadsheet is free, offline, exports anywhere. But a row that says
        "$68 fuel" isn't proof you spent it &mdash; the receipt is.
      </p>
      <p class="measure">
        Keep the receipt; pair it with your sheet or skip it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The gap</p>
      <h2>The sheet assumes<br />the paper survives.</h2>
      <p class="intro measure">
        Every row in a receipt spreadsheet is a pointer. It says: on this date, at
        this vendor, I spent this much &mdash; and the document proving it is
        somewhere else. In a folder, a drawer, a glovebox.
      </p>
      <p class="measure">
        That pointer is only worth as much as the thing it points at. Fuel slips,
        scale tickets, and truck stop receipts are thermal paper, and thermal paper
        in a hot cab goes blank in weeks. The row stays perfectly legible while the
        document behind it turns into a white rectangle.
      </p>
      <p class="measure">
        The spreadsheet does not know that happened. It looks exactly as complete
        the day the receipt fades as it did the day you typed it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The other gap</p>
      <h2>Typing at the pump<br />does not happen.</h2>
      <p class="intro measure">
        A spreadsheet needs two hands, a keyboard or a very patient thumb, and a
        moment of calm. None of those exist at a fuel island with the engine
        running and someone waiting behind you.
      </p>
      <p class="measure">
        So the entry gets deferred. The paper goes in the door pocket to be typed
        up later, and later is where receipts go to die. Not because anyone is
        careless &mdash; because the batch keeps growing and the paper keeps fading
        while it waits.
      </p>
      <p class="measure">
        A photo takes three seconds and does not need a free hand for a keyboard.
        That is the entire difference in the habit.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Together</p>
      <h2>Keep the sheet<br />if you like it.</h2>
      <p class="intro measure">
        This is not really a versus. A spreadsheet does arithmetic; CabSnap keeps
        evidence. If you want the running total in a sheet, keep the running total
        in a sheet.
      </p>
      <p class="measure">
        Just make sure something, somewhere, is holding an image of the paper each
        row is pointing at &mdash; because in April the row is not the thing anyone
        wants to see.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Questions</p>
      <h2>Straight answers.</h2>
      <Faq items={faq} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <Closing html="Keep the row.<br />Keep the <em>receipt</em>." />
</Base>

<style>
  .hero { padding: 56px 0 52px; }
  .eyebrow.orange { color: var(--orange); }
  h1 em, h2 em { font-style: normal; color: var(--orange); }
  .lead { font-size: 18px; margin-top: 18px; }
  .intro { margin-top: 16px; }
  section p + p { margin-top: 14px; }

  .dark :global(.faq details) { border-bottom-color: var(--rule-dk); }
  .dark :global(.faq summary) { color: var(--paper); }
  .dark :global(.faq summary::after) { color: var(--green); }
  @media (min-width: 1024px) { .hero { padding: 80px 0; } }
</style>
